---
// Lesson 11.3 — Written-Response Practice (PPR in hand)
// assessment: WR drills with PPR; the 4 exam-day prompts; timing.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>11.3 — Written-Response Practice (PPR in hand)</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The exam's written section gives you 60 minutes for 4 prompts,
    and the only notes allowed are the PPR you built
    (<a href="11.2a.html">11.2a</a>). Today you rehearse the real
    conditions: prompts unseen, PPR in hand, clock running — and
    you learn the sentence shapes the rubrics reward.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Drill One</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The exam-day WR structure (different from your PT
    prompts — see the
    <a href="../exam/written-responses.html">full guide</a>):</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>WR1:</strong> YOUR program, with your PPR —
            name a specific output and explain how it shows
            what your program does</li>
        <li><strong>WR2a/2b/2c:</strong> YOUR program, with your
            PPR: algorithm explanation, list usage, and
            development difficulty + fix</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>The shapes that score:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Claim + evidence + because:</strong> "The
            procedure returns -1 when the list is empty BECAUSE
            the loop never executes and the initial value of
            total is returned."</li>
        <li><strong>Name the mechanism, not the vibe:</strong>
            "sequencing because lines 2→3 depend on each other"
            beats "it does things in order."</li>
        <li><strong>For your PPR answers:</strong> quote your own
            variable names. "my inventory list" scores;
            "my list" scores less.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Drill (15 minutes, timed):</strong> with your PPR
    open beside you exactly as Bluebook will show it, write WR2a
    (algorithm) and WR2c (list + procedure) from scratch, exam
    style. Swap with a partner: does THEIR rubric read (does the
    evidence quote real code lines) hold up? Full rubrics on the
    <a href="../exam/written-responses.html">guide page</a>.</p>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Drill Two</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Read code cold (the multiple-choice skill).</strong>
    Here is a foreign program, exam-style — describe its behavior
    in three sentences or fewer:</p>
    <pre><code>PROCEDURE mystery(list, target)
{
    count ← 0
    FOR EACH item IN list
    {
        IF (item &gt; target)
        {
            count ← count + 1
        }
    }
    RETURN count
}</code></pre>
    <p>Write: input (___ and ___), behavior (___), output (___).
    Then the version the multiple-choice section asks: what does
    mystery(list, 0) return for a list of all negative numbers?
    What about an empty list — trace the loop count.</p>


    <div class="socratic">


        <h3>Socratic: Under the Clock, PPR in Hand</h3>


        <ol>


            <li>You answered with the PPR in hand and the clock running. Which prompt did the clock change your answer to, and would the unhurried version have been better?</li>


            <li>A peer scored you against the rubric rows. Which row did you think you had earned, and the rubric disagreed about?</li>


            <li>The prompts were unseen. What is the one sentence you would write first for any prompt, to keep the rest of the answer honest?</li>


        </ol>


    </div>



    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Write claim+evidence answers quoting real variable names</li>
            <li>Describe foreign code's input/behavior/output in 3 sentences</li>
            <li>Answer WR2b with the specific bug-cause-fix triple</li>
            <li>Finish a timed WR2a+2c drill inside 15 minutes</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../exam/written-responses.html">Written Responses</a> — the exam-day prompts and rubrics</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/reference-sheet.html">Exam Reference Sheet</a> — pseudocode you must read fluently</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="11.4a.html">11.4a — MC Strategy + Practice Set 1</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="11.3" title="11.3 Written-Response Practice (PPR in hand) — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
